  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:  \nThe problem is a nonconvex optimization with box and equality constraints, requiring minimization of the maximum cross-correlation (overlap) integral over all lags. Global optimality is elusive due to high dimensionality (400 variables) and nonconvexity. Current local methods (`SLSQP`, `subgradient_polish`) are effective but stall within local optima. We adopt a hierarchical strategy: coarse-to-fine resolution (16 \u2192 256 points) uses semidefinite programming (SDP) for initial feasibility, optimized annealing schedules in the medium-resolution phase, and targeted gradient-based polishing. Continuation methods progressively refine the solution. This leverages structure: the problem is separable in the time domain, and early-stage SDP-based feasibility pruning reduces dimensionality.\n\nMathematical specification:  \n**Variables:** Piecewise-affine \\(h \\in \\mathbb{R}_+^{n}\\) for \\(x \\in \\{0, \\frac{2}{n-1}, \\ldots, 2\\}\\), satisfying \\(0 \\leq h_i \\leq 1\\) and \\(\\sum_{i=1}^{n} h_i \\cdot \\frac{2}{n-1} \\approx 1\\) (adjusted by clipping).  \n**Objective:** \\(\\min h \\text{ subject to } C_5(h) \\leq T\\), where \\(T\\) is the target bound (initially 0.382, then lowered to 0.3805).\n**Constraints:** Linear box constraints and an equality \\(\\sum h_i \\cdot \\frac{2}{n-1} = \\frac{n}{2}\\).  \n**Core Objective:** \\(\\max_{k=-n+1}^{n-1} \\left| \\sum_{i} h_i \\cdot (1 - h_{i+k}) \\cdot \\frac{2}{n-1} \\right|\\).  \n**Algorithm:** Coarse-to-fine: Start with \\(n=16\\), use SDP to enforce feasibility (clipping-free) and set target \\(T_1\\). Proceed to medium \\(n=64\\), then high \\(n=256\\) (search space \\(0 \\leq h_i \\leq 1\\)). Objective computed via `compute_corr` scaled by \\(dx=2.0/n\\).\n\nImplementation procedure:  \n1. **Parent Handling:** Validate initial `h_values` (400 pt) by projecting to resolution \\(n\\): interpolate/clip to meet constraints. Feasibility via `project_feasible`.  \n2. **Resolution Transition:** From coarse (\\(n=16\\)) to medium (\\(n=64\\)), scale `initial_h_values` via linear interpolation, then optimize.  \n3. **Core Optimization:**\tUse Bayesian optimization with annealing (target temperature schedule) and Gaussian processes (for continuation).  \n4. **Gradient Estimate:** Numerical gradient (central difference) via `compute_obj_grad(h)`:  \n   \\[\n   \\nabla C_5(h) \\approx \\frac{1}{\\epsilon} \\left[ C_5(h + \\epsilon e_i) - C_5(h - \\epsilon e_i) \\right]\n   \\]\n   with \\(\\epsilon=0.001\\).  \n5. **Constraint Enforcement:** Post-evaluation projection to \\([0,1]\\) and sum constraint (\\(dx \\cdot \\sum h = 1\\)).\n\nSearch choices and variations:  \n- **Initial Annealing:** Start at high \\(T\\) (e.g., 0.45), lower systematically (temperature schedule).  \n- **Resolution Adaptation:** If plateauing, refine early via \\(SDP \\rightarrow 64 \\rightarrow 256\\).  \n- **Feasibility Handling:** Priority on constraints, avoid domain-capping.  \n- **Stopping:** Record best value, exit if \\(n\\)-step improvement fails (locality check).\n\nComputebudget and robustness:  \n- **Phase Allocation:** Coarse (500s), medium (600s), fine (200s).  \n- **Retry Mechanism:** On failure, adjust `epsilon` in gradients.  \n- **Fallback:** Persist best candidate if time/temperature exhausted.  \n- **Validation:** Numeric checks: sum(h) \u2248 n/2, all \\(h_i \\in [0,1]\\), \\(C_5 \\neq \\infty\\).\n\nValidation and return contract:  \n- Validate candidate via `compute_c5(h)`.  \n- Output: `(h_values, c5_bound, n_points)` with `c5_bound = minimal feasible T`.  \n- Generate one-time check via `worst_lag_surgery` for tightness.",
